<header class="page-head"><div class="eyebrow">Official sources, defined methods</div><h1>Hail Data Sources and Methodology</h1><p>Peak Country identifies each weather source, separates current information from historical records, and explains what the data can and cannot establish.</p></header>
<article class="section narrow prose">
<h2>NOAA/NCEI Storm Events Database</h2><p>The historical archive uses the <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/stormevents/">NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information Storm Events Database</a> bulk files. Peak Country selects records identified as Hail, validates required fields, and retains the official event identifiers and source links. The public archive currently covers the ten completed seasons from 2016 through 2025. The current 2026 season is excluded from the completed-decade totals because current-year data can remain provisional.</p><p>NOAA can correct or revise historical Storm Events records after publication. The repository records the precise annual archive filenames used so results can be reproduced and later reconciled against a newer corrected release. Peak Country does not rewrite an observation to fill a missing magnitude or location.</p>
<h2>National Weather Service</h2><p>The <a href="https://api.weather.gov/alerts/active?area=CO">National Weather Service active-alert API</a> provides severe-weather information, including warning geometry and affected areas where supplied. The live tracker prioritizes relevant alert polygons near the Greeley-centered area instead of depending entirely on county-name matching. Broader Colorado alerts remain secondary so distant warnings do not dominate the Northern Colorado view.</p><p>Always follow official NWS warnings and local emergency instructions during severe weather. Peak Country’s site is an informational repair and hail-report resource, not an emergency warning system.</p>
<h2>Storm Prediction Center</h2><p>The <a href="https://www.spc.noaa.gov/climo/reports/today_hail.csv">NOAA Storm Prediction Center preliminary hail-report feed</a> provides current context. These observations can be useful soon after a storm, but preliminary reports may be delayed, updated, or corrected. The tracker labels its source and refresh status and does not invent a report when the feed is unavailable or empty.</p>
<h2>Greeley anchor and 50-mile filter</h2><p>The historical process uses the Greeley city geographic reference point at latitude {manifest.geographic_filter.latitude} and longitude {manifest.geographic_filter.longitude}. This is a calculation anchor, not a Peak Country address or shop location. Qualifying observations must have valid coordinates and fall within 50 statute miles.</p><p>Distance is calculated with the Haversine formula, a great-circle calculation using latitude and longitude on the Earth’s surface. This produces a consistent straight-line geographic distance. A 50-mile circle crosses municipal and county boundaries, so regional records are not automatically labeled as belonging to Greeley or Weld County. The Weld County view uses NOAA’s source area field for county attribution.</p>
<h2>Reports, hail days, and storm episodes</h2><p>A hail report is one qualifying official observation. A hail day is one calendar date with at least one qualifying observation. Several reports can occur on the same day, and several observations can belong to one NOAA episode. Peak Country therefore does not present report count as storm count. Annual and decade summaries are calculated from the generated dataset and validated against the published CSV, JSON, and summary files.</p>
<h2>How cached and unavailable data is handled</h2><p>Official feeds can fail temporarily. When retained data is shown, the tracker labels its timestamp and stale condition rather than presenting it as current. A source failure produces an unavailable state instead of simulated observations. Meaningful page text and source explanations remain available even when a live request cannot be completed.</p>
<h2>Freshness and limitations</h2><p>Live pages display source status and distinguish current, stale cached, and unavailable data. Reports can be delayed or incomplete, and location accuracy varies by observer and source. An empty feed does not prove that no hail occurred. A nearby hail report confirms only that hail was reported in the area; it does not prove that a specific vehicle was exposed or damaged. A direct vehicle inspection is required.</p><p>Explore the <a href="/hail-tracker/">Live Hail Tracker</a>, search the <a href="/northern-colorado-hail-history/">verified historical archive</a>, or review the <a href="/hail-size-guide/">Hail Size Guide</a>. Technical details are preserved in the archive source manifest and repository validation tests.</p>
